WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:12.570
Hi everyone it's Jerry. On November 24th, 2012
Magnus Carlsen competed in a game against

00:00:12.629 --> 00:00:19.039
the world in the 2nd Gran chess festival held
in Mexico City, Mexico. For each of the moves

00:00:19.039 --> 00:00:25.320
the world voted for one of three options that
were selected by grandmaster players. And

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:32.320
in this game Carlsen, on the white end, kicked
off with e4. And what was voted on was the

00:00:32.470 --> 00:00:39.470
Sicilian c5. After knight f3 d6 were heading
for an open Sicilian with d4. cxd Nxd knight

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:48.180
f6, knight c3 and now a6 indicating the Najdorf
and on move six h3. What's also quite popular

00:00:51.350 --> 00:00:58.350
is bishop e3 and after e5 the knight will
make use of that b3 square. However with h3,

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:05.800
white is intending on g4 bishop g2 ideas and
a rerouting of this knight to this f5 square.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:14.500
So that's exactly what we have. After e5 it's
not the knight going to b3 but rather e2.

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:21.210
Bishop e7, g4 expanding, you'll note that this
is a strong support point on f5. This knight is 

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:27.310
on its way there. Black castles, knight g3
black gets some development in connecting

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:32.820
their knights and after knight f5 is now in
a position to just move this knight out of

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:39.320
the way and it's quite timely. This d-pawn
is now defended and there's now pressure on

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:46.320
this e4 point. Bishop g2 development defense
of e4. More development on the black side

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:53.420
and now castles. After b5 we have a3. This
knight does not want to be kicked around and

00:01:54.259 --> 00:02:01.259
so rook c8 half open file very logical moves
and now f4 striking at this center and has

00:02:02.619 --> 00:02:07.649
the potential to enter the black territory.
After this knight moves this pawn could be

00:02:07.649 --> 00:02:12.740
expanding and that's exactly what we would
have, white establishing a pawn in the black

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:19.740
position. So what follows is knight a4 which
is a curious move to see because you do run

00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:27.930
the risk of...well a messy looking pawn structure.
But the idea here is to eliminate this knight

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:37.550
on c3 which is better placed than this knight
on c5(c6). Okay, you could reason this knight    

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:43.320
is applying pressure to this pawn but there's
not really a way to put additional pressure

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:49.790
on e4 and if you recognize the weakest point
in the black position, the weakest square

00:02:49.790 --> 00:02:55.500
d5, you really want to try and get rid of
a knight that could potentially make use of

00:02:55.500 --> 00:03:02.500
it. So knight a4 addresses this idea and although
black has doubled a-pawns, it now allows black

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:12.780
to put pressure on this b-pawn. And well we're
not going to see this right away but the potential

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:18.950
to put pressure on the heart of this white
queen side structure is there. And if pressure

00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:24.730
is placed on b2, it may prove to be quite
uncomfortable for white to complete their

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:31.460
queen side development. So first things first
we have some expansion, a lot of action actually

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:38.460
on the king side and a lot of structural changes
within these next few moves, and most of which

00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:45.750
are are all forced. After g5 knight d7 knight
takes bishop, queen recaptures, and now f5

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:52.060
hitting that bishop, it only has one choice.
It reacts counterattacking move hitting the

00:03:52.060 --> 00:03:57.720
rook and the rook just bumps up to f2. And
black has to be very careful right now because

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:04.720
the main idea is to just a rip open this king
side structure by making this pawn on g7 flinch,

00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:11.230
or in other words trying to get in f6, which
is often recognized as a giant thorn in one's

00:04:11.230 --> 00:04:18.230
position. So black does well to avoid this
by playing f6, and now we have g6. A lot of

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:26.250
tension going on with these last couple of
moves between the pawns, and the decision

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:33.250
here now by black is a great one. Because
there is the option of taking the pawn and

00:04:34.370 --> 00:04:41.370
after the recapture, working with this particular
structure, or just playing h6. Now what was

00:04:43.340 --> 00:04:49.460
decided on was h6 and we'll see how this now
turns out to be a great inconvenience to black

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:56.460
because this pawn is just priceless. Controlling
these two flight squares it will always present

00:04:57.220 --> 00:05:04.220
black a problem for as long as there is one
heavy piece on the board. There's the potential

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:11.360
for back rank issues, and we'll see that highlighted
later on. But the other alternative...okay

00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:17.300
what was played was h6. The other alternative
it to actually capture on g6, and at first

00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:24.300
I thought that this would be a mistake because
what it now allows white to do, or so I thought,

00:05:25.820 --> 00:05:32.560
what it now allows white to do is play queen
h5, and then make use of this h7 square. But

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:38.390
black, fortunately, would be in time to meet
that threat. After h takes g, what we could

00:05:38.390 --> 00:05:44.590
have or what we should have is rook f to    	
d8 which is vacating f8 to meet a move such

00:05:44.590 --> 00:05:50.800
as queen h5 with knight f8, and black is there
just in the nick of time to not only defend

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:57.800
h7 but the other idea is that it's putting
pressure on g6. And so things aren't now as

00:05:59.810 --> 00:06:06.170
clean for white. That's not to say that this
is the best continuation, but just to highlight

00:06:06.170 --> 00:06:10.110
how black would be defending should queen
h5 be played: the knight would make use of

00:06:10.110 --> 00:06:14.389
f8, defend against h7, and apply pressure
to this pawn which runs the risk of just falling.    

00:06:14.389 --> 00:06:19.389
Not long off, we could be seeing this queen     

00:06:19.389 --> 00:06:26.020
e8 move and white is having to find some accurate
moves to be defending against this, and in

00:06:26.020 --> 00:06:31.220
the meantime there could still be mounting
pressure against the white queen side. So,

00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:35.020
we didn't have this but this is a great turning
point in the game and that will often spring

00:06:35.020 --> 00:06:42.020
about when we have structural changes. So
h6 was played. There is no potential now to

00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:49.160
get to that h7 square with the queen. So things
now transition since the king side is locked.

00:06:49.160 --> 00:06:55.490
We're now transitioning to the queen side.
So bishop e3 is played just completing...

00:06:55.490 --> 00:06:59.880
getting more pieces involved. And the           
other idea that white maybe could have gone

00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:06.880
with is b3. Taking note that all of these
squares are just taken away from that light-squared

00:07:06.980 --> 00:07:12.830
bishop. And so the bishop will be kicked off
of it after either an immediate capture, or

00:07:12.830 --> 00:07:17.560
just going back right away. Let's say takes
takes the bishop kind of gets kicked around

00:07:17.560 --> 00:07:24.560
for a bunch of moves here. a4 bishop c6, and
after b4 white will not be denied of getting

00:07:24.639 --> 00:07:31.639
to this nice diagonal and development is really
really smooth. If let's say king h8, in anticipation

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:38.680
of a queen playing to b3, we just have really
smooth development. And the big thing here

00:07:38.680 --> 00:07:43.110
is that this pawn here is still a liability,
and these pawns are perfectly healthy and

00:07:43.110 --> 00:07:50.110
soon enough can be converted into a passed
pawn. So that was another route white could

00:07:50.530 --> 00:07:56.360
have gone for as soon as h6 was played...
to play b3, but we didn't have that. Instead

00:07:56.360 --> 00:08:03.360
it was bishop e3, knight c5 repositioning.
Rook d2, it only came there to meet that bishop

00:08:04.030 --> 00:08:09.350
threat when it played to that c4 square. So
now it's functioning very well placing pressure

00:08:09.350 --> 00:08:16.350
on d6, that rooks gets involved to defend.
Queen e1 so that these rooks can now double.

00:08:16.530 --> 00:08:21.190
Queen b7. And I was talking earlier about
the rook maybe putting pressure on b2, but

00:08:21.190 --> 00:08:27.130
sure enough we can also have that queen playing
the role. White avoids this not wanting to

00:08:27.130 --> 00:08:33.640
play a very passive move. Rook b1 is just
not the way to go. It could always be let's

00:08:33.640 --> 00:08:38.110
say kicked away. There's not a convenient
way to defend against b2, so the idea here

00:08:38.110 --> 00:08:45.110
is to put pressure on c6. And the big thing
here is really all about activity. Queen takes

00:08:46.690 --> 00:08:52.520
b2 was played in other words because trying
to hang on to let's say this pawn on d6 just

00:08:52.520 --> 00:08:57.560
runs into some trouble. I mean black is on
the defensive in a great way. What do you

00:08:57.560 --> 00:09:03.770
do after something like queen to f2, putting
more pressure on the knight, with still ideas

00:09:03.770 --> 00:09:10.480
of penetrating into this back rank of black's?
It's very very difficult. Keep in mind that

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:16.830
a move such as knight takes e is not available
because of queen f3. What do you do about

00:09:16.830 --> 00:09:22.320
this pin? There's not a good solution. If
d5, you can actually just take this knight.

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:27.890
Again, taking advantage of this back rank.
This would be the best choice for black at

00:09:27.890 --> 00:09:32.350
that point, to immediately play the queen
back e8. But after the smoke clears, white

00:09:32.350 --> 00:09:39.350
is clearly just up a minor piece. So, we wouldn't
have knight takes e at this point. The main

00:09:40.890 --> 00:09:46.950
idea again is not to just go and resort to
a completely defensive position. I guess you

00:09:46.950 --> 00:09:52.990
could play queen c6 after queen f2, the knight
going backwards just to defend the pawn, but

00:09:52.990 --> 00:09:58.900
that's not really the way to play the game.
So instead opting for activity, grabbing on

00:09:58.900 --> 00:10:03.930
b2, and is just a step away from grabbing
a3 and this pawn becomes passed. But there

00:10:03.930 --> 00:10:10.570
is some serious issues again, all really attributed
to this pawn being so deep in the black position,

00:10:10.570 --> 00:10:16.430
controlling these flight squares. So, rook
takes d, rook takes d, rook takes d, and now

00:10:16.430 --> 00:10:22.160
queen to a3. This pawn is passed and this
queen is indirectly observing that rook on

00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:29.160
d6. Queen d2, renewing this idea of getting to 
the back rank. Knight d3, an interference

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:35.070
move. There's a disconnect now between the
queen and the rook. The queen is hitting the

00:10:35.070 --> 00:10:41.899
rook. And we have rook d7, probably the most
active square for that rook right now. Bishop

00:10:41.899 --> 00:10:48.899
b5 hits the rook. Rook d5, and now knight
to f4 hitting this rook. Rook c5 is a cute

00:10:50.440 --> 00:10:56.350
move just interfering. Black just a moment
ago played that knight to d3 move interfering

00:10:56.350 --> 00:11:00.960
with the queen and rook's connection. And we     
have a similar thing going on right now...

00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:05.740
the rook is getting in the way of the queen
coming back to maybe defend along this diagonal.

00:11:05.740 --> 00:11:10.890
And of course if rook takes, we're just going
to have this mate. Once again everything is

00:11:10.890 --> 00:11:16.040
highlighted as soon as one of the pieces is
no longer around to watch over that eighth

00:11:16.040 --> 00:11:23.040
rank, white can simply exploit it. So, after
rook c5 we have rook e8. And there are alternatives

00:11:23.990 --> 00:11:29.730
here... there's many different choices for
the rook, but with each of them white is always

00:11:29.730 --> 00:11:36.350
slightly better, if not winning There's maybe
some more resistance that could be put up,

00:11:36.350 --> 00:11:41.839
but what was chosen on this move thirty was
rook to e8, and now this is really going to

00:11:41.839 --> 00:11:46.770
crumble very fast. What white could have played,
what Magnus could've played is an immediate

00:11:46.770 --> 00:11:52.320
c4, knowing that if let's say the bishop plays
to d7, white can actually go ahead and grab

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:59.190
it. And allow a very appealing looking queen
takes bishop with check, giving a fork to

00:11:59.190 --> 00:12:05.520
the king and rook. That can just be met with
king h2, and there's no time to take the rook

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:12.520
because we would just have check followed
by mate in one. So, there is just a little

00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:22.600
bit of a long winded variation here. After
king h2, there's not much you can do about

00:12:22.740 --> 00:12:28.800
the rook being threatened. And also this idea
of bringing the rook to maybe c7 or c8. If

00:12:28.800 --> 00:12:35.120
rook f8 is tried, rook c8. What to do about
taking the rook and giving mate here or, just

00:12:35.120 --> 00:12:41.360
giving check first and then taking the rook?
There's not much you can do. Knight g6, pawn

00:12:41.360 --> 00:12:46.680
takes knight. There's just a couple checks
that could be thrown in here and eventually

00:12:46.680 --> 00:12:53.680
the king is going to find a safe haven on
this h5 square, and this idea of the back

00:12:53.800 --> 00:13:00.800
rank is still present. So what we do have
instead of this c4 move immediately, this

00:13:02.640 --> 00:13:07.440
is what could have been played successfully,
but there is a certain degree of calculation

00:13:07.440 --> 00:13:13.860
involved knowing that you can avoid let's
say that perpetual check. Instead of c4, we

00:13:13.860 --> 00:13:20.860
do have rook to c7, getting back on that seventh
rank. Queen gives check, king h2, and now

00:13:21.970 --> 00:13:27.820
king to h8. There's not much that black can
do right here. There's the idea with king

00:13:27.820 --> 00:13:32.700
h8 is just to get off of this diagonal so
that it, you know, when the queen... if the

00:13:32.700 --> 00:13:38.950
queen does land on that diagonal, it's not
going to come with check. If for example,

00:13:38.950 --> 00:13:43.390
instead of the king going in the corner, if
we had the pawn just trying to promote...

00:13:43.390 --> 00:13:48.760
well we snip the knight, pawn takes bishop,
queen gives check, king in the corner, queen

00:13:48.760 --> 00:13:54.250
f7. What do you do about the pressure on g7?
Not much to do. Rook g8, you just take the

00:13:54.250 --> 00:14:01.250
rook, and then you deliver mate in two. So,
one other variation might be, after this position,

00:14:05.860 --> 00:14:12.620
after king h2 excuse me, queen a2, preventing
let's say c2 from coming about...

00:14:12.620 --> 00:14:18.230
we could have queen to d6. That's not to say
that this is the best move in this position,

00:14:18.230 --> 00:14:25.019
but just to highlight the strength of this
pawn right here on g6. It's not just around

00:14:25.019 --> 00:14:31.029
to provide mate on the back rank, but there
are tactics involved against the g7 point

00:14:31.029 --> 00:14:37.100
as well. If for example a3 just trying again
to promote, this is the tactic that we would

00:14:37.100 --> 00:14:42.970
have: rook takes g, king takes g, queen c7,
king back, the queen gives check. And now

00:14:42.970 --> 00:14:49.019
this bishop starts to play a role. And this
will be leading to mate in a couple. So, however

00:14:49.019 --> 00:14:56.019
you slice it, after queen a1, for sure, black
is completely busted. So, queen a1, king h2,

00:14:57.170 --> 00:15:04.110
the king gets off of this diagonal. And what
follows is c4. This bishop is killed. And,

00:15:04.110 --> 00:15:09.760
what black tries is just to advance the pawn.
There's not much more that could be done.

00:15:09.760 --> 00:15:16.760
So Magnus grabs that bishop, queen b2, pawn
grabs on a6 and well this guy is just a couple

00:15:17.330 --> 00:15:21.769
steps away and there's just nothing to do
for black at this point. What is tried is

00:15:21.769 --> 00:15:28.769
queen takes queen, bishop takes queen, knight
e2. And after a calm rook c4, a2 and rook

00:15:31.060 --> 00:15:36.670
to a4, this pawn is lost, this pawn remains
passed, and white is still up a piece. Black

00:15:36.670 --> 00:15:42.310
is dead. And at this point the world ends
up resigning to the world's number one, Magnus

00:15:42.310 --> 00:15:47.730
Carlsen. So, that's all for this video. As
always, I hope you got something out of it.

00:15:47.730 --> 00:15:48.520
Take care, bye.

